  <div class="abstract">Exact worst-case reconstruction of a binary word from independent deletion traces requires \(n^{\Omega(\log\log n)}\) samples for every fixed deletion probability \(q\in(0,1)\), even with unrestricted computation and any fixed positive success probability. This gives a negative answer to the polynomial-sample question for binary trace reconstruction. More generally, when \(q^3\log n\to\infty\), we prove a lower bound of \(n^{c\log(q^3\log n)}\) samples for every fixed \(0\lt c\lt 1/(4\log2)\). Here <i>q</i> is the known deletion probability, and all logarithms are natural.</div>
